#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128790 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/id #uuid "64c86335-cc36-5b99-a8e3-f2658435b29b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12401" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/5a19eefe8fdcd63ef9f8a35bf4517b022ab2c35b5fccd06e1e797f67e567653a.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R  f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_0^1\\int_{\\sqrt{z}}^1\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/ec195fe4aa1bec5221ec25a1921dfe36e78db34ad3e295fec541c1d05a85d60d.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{z}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nz = y^2\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z\\leq y^2 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/841f014cae7bb224b6f743a86583cb0a70a423d60176b2efd6482d155aa020bc.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\n\\int_0^1 \\int_{0}^{y^2} \\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128792 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/id #uuid "f8b32b74-b82a-5c07-b244-945189e2bcc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12403" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_{-1}^0 \\int_{y^2}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\n+\n\\int_0^1 \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)}  f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R = R_1\\cup R_2,$ where $R_1$ and $R_2$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x-1\\le z\\le {y}^{2}}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x-1\\le z\\le {y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c8bfd933472e1944f63b1295bda7fc324311ea2ca2ada3636e9f5ab82e4e5fc5.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type II regions in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD =\n\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 0, \\quad y^2 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n\\:\\cup\\:\n\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the lower boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny = -\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand the upper boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=y\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny =x.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad -\\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq  x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c73a0e30d6996490f9f41bf3e8777c14d542755387f643ad464bd1dd6cab2839.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-\\sqrt{x}}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x}} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128793 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/id #uuid "72795f5f-0d15-52e1-bfd8-997ae8e20265" :question/math-academy-id "e-12404" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: Change change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: y \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq y \\right\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/bdd856ef8a7940a009b7ee27d6614fdb8c042e6655c2316ce4db0633bfd04e9f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y = y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx = y \\qquad\\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny = 0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/1e28e1cef9743da80d6fd2d38840810b12239d8546254c4d4cca767568f76a53.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{y}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}[z]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3y{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{{x}^{3}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac67bf19-0354-51fd-b6ba-285382f59111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x^{2}+y}}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126585} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1eb3ad-69a6-549c-b927-6b926ee6e1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126586} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f8c9f-f08e-5430-a8a7-80876465704c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126587} {:answer/id #uuid "29a769e1-3aa3-5db1-9000-5f9d5ffe61ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{y^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126588} {:answer/id #uuid "412f00e2-be13-5c92-8b02-4cbe54888137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x^{2}+y}}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f8c9f-f08e-5430-a8a7-80876465704c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126587} :answer-field/id #uuid "683adc15-d582-59cc-a8c2-3bc724db908e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "318d728a-5f9a-5d33-846c-76ae520cec78" :question/math-academy-id "q-162226" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c420d45ca9418fd29889d91a49cce4ae80bb71bdfd4e4aa701b495cc8d87335f.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/8b7cfa72ed3dbfb121039c6e64b843d83489878a9f457fb1386e455e7d9649ec.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/f49af5da2e4f1f281700e16c4659fabfcd0b15661947ca985d38f208f8563877.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdbcaf83-da40-5234-9510-51e69fe5835b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377633} {:answer/id #uuid "b437d7dc-1023-56f7-937d-07672c7853b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{2y}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377634} {:answer/id #uuid "1d710a62-b8f9-558c-9da9-8b32890c3443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377635} {:answer/id #uuid "da136f4b-724e-5ff0-bb16-c264e62ae9aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}}^{{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377636} {:answer/id #uuid "fcab31e8-0fe9-5418-a2f2-69949e373c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcab31e8-0fe9-5418-a2f2-69949e373c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377637} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca93b573-3535-5adc-b8bb-70b848a49bc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4106b43e-8253-5efa-befa-b666867a250d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162247" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-2{x}^{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2x}}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,-2{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,-y\\le z\\le x+3}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\sqrt{2x}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,-y\\le z\\le x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/834238127fb9a55c956b2dd708e1dc7d925c63725990003eff6aac28ee42a873.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type I regions in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-2{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 2}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2x}\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-2{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=-\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/82bddc0c83e0e5a5824b7525bcd4fd2dc9b8f88473dfe1fd5c6cdb2ff0cefcaf.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b5888b7-bb23-55c1-9a63-516940c0e6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{z-2}^{6-z}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377640} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a9e557-2530-5927-a4a0-ad2020f69af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377641} {:answer/id #uuid "877503cb-db2e-5625-809b-452cd3651fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2{z}^{2}}^{6+{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377642} {:answer/id #uuid "7c51c0ac-4407-509c-817e-da7c3867c125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}+1}^{6-\\sqrt{z}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377643} {:answer/id #uuid "a365e93b-3efe-5602-8e93-8cbc2b4df1e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a365e93b-3efe-5602-8e93-8cbc2b4df1e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377644} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4561445-190b-5a65-b1b6-e62169f33c9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8f2ddb0-6f04-5162-bb72-1102d26c5f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162249" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y+2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6-y}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y+2},\\,\\,-z\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 6,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{6-y},\\,\\,-z\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/aa1c884900c3cb01a719dc6d22604469984d2b08291d9d925b8f37d794d455b2.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type I regions in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y+2}}\\,\\cup \\,{(y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 6,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{6-y}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y+2}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{6-y}\\,⟹\\,y=6-{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,{z}^{2}-2\\le y\\le 6-{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/e4139dde4d56e83b91e4a72d42ccdf296ad20773520ccdaa51d7a84b9e1a3f31.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268aa011-3831-5afd-81d3-e0cdc5b4836c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377647} {:answer/id #uuid "50158ac7-a0fb-50e7-8725-e02a68e224d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377648} {:answer/id #uuid "4a94cfe0-ad28-5d61-90ca-a16610dfe2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377649} {:answer/id #uuid "dbed0b99-4f1f-5461-a071-1f46c8d229df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377650} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb7abb0-97a7-581b-82f1-ae23e7072e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50158ac7-a0fb-50e7-8725-e02a68e224d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377648} :answer-field/id #uuid "043a2b4c-9817-578b-9e14-4c244bf9436a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c90887-d6f5-52fb-a567-57a97fdd9e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162251" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/62377f3e744c5767927f9591b8c6e3335a8036cad5fe9c62f52c041b2db2bac8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/05de07930f6cacfd0a3a21045a95628891965bb69d5df1bef4dbeeab9e480b97.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[x]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8335bf15-b7ec-5325-b78e-8b855d45c996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126591} {:answer/id #uuid "a0303244-9248-5829-b1aa-b977399244bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126592} {:answer/id #uuid "efdb629a-bfb1-5457-bb56-973b2bbdb505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126593} {:answer/id #uuid "18cde4fd-b4ae-510c-951a-1b51325c7b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126594} {:answer/id #uuid "44d12730-261e-5f3c-a3e4-4026931916f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efdb629a-bfb1-5457-bb56-973b2bbdb505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126593} :answer-field/id #uuid "365cbf84-2149-55dd-8287-bd41d84dd05e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c00cd44a-d34c-5f6e-aacf-b4ef475837d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162253" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {e}^{z/x}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/e83c249a6abf9710a201462e16c646a11411dbcdbca46f34e9beb8d2db7a654b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{z}\\,⟹\\,z={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/7e684766b85a214d1f1dd7845979052c532e11ecce74d0284b8fc379b5669957.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}[y]_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{e}^{z/x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{e}^{z/x}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{e}^{z/x}]_{z=0}^{z={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{e}^{x}-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[(x-1){e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{1}{2})-(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "605ca4f3-911f-5d13-981a-5dd9a9e65158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi -1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377654} {:answer/id #uuid "d6291363-af79-5793-9520-a90330f1a0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377655} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3bdbbb-093c-51cb-a70e-7d83bcdd7f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377656} {:answer/id #uuid "15cf675f-e1a7-506c-9e30-cb2cc1691995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377657} {:answer/id #uuid "5c69b8e4-f0bb-541e-9b5d-2120d414fba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee3bdbbb-093c-51cb-a70e-7d83bcdd7f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377656} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a8bed3e-1303-5408-be69-4b8ad84a24c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "453e7dea-796b-536b-8d7e-c85a4912bef8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162330" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c5f4ec9b9b12411f3057bc074a14a1f541c768c30d3a9746d7a0484b0491ad2e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/077fc8133673cd57aba570cca9a6e0f5f25aa742720948c46db2b9e1d05add69.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}y\\,[x]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[z+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a754e93d-4ae6-5bfb-8051-569d258516f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377661} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec7ce88-c36c-538b-a43f-aff00b324b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377662} {:answer/id #uuid "ad99d3e6-6909-5d12-ab7d-1b4f972bb4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377663} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6249a5-f3b8-5047-9960-df2c2d5f1737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(2\\pi +1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377664} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8882fb-b29d-526b-9d62-9d82fd346ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ec7ce88-c36c-538b-a43f-aff00b324b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377662} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e25978-248e-534d-9a1c-4cdc63b20e94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b03f14a-f205-5f36-8c12-2700a8eeba99" :question/math-academy-id "q-162331" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2yz}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/d3865f790df8c2a228f08c290a113954687ade63a275a754c4cb3199c234d5ec.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\,⟹\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/085b2ce5a9ecd2632f70f287bbef5e9f04566871fc8adf7ba10076e2e875ca2e.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}{[-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})]}_{x=0}^{x=2yz}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}(-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y+z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}z(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y)\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[y-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y]}_{0}^{\\pi /4}-\\frac{1}{12}[{sin}^{3}⁡2y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)-\\frac{1}{12}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27050d71-02ed-5b34-ab26-8dc8c616fc11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377668} {:answer/id #uuid "afbee862-c6a3-5534-8218-c408b82f6705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377669} {:answer/id #uuid "e127bfc0-6417-5f15-860e-708803c53bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377670} {:answer/id #uuid "cf423ee9-81a0-54f8-bffc-325786a0d5c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=3,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377671} {:answer/id #uuid "600b42f6-ffe3-5637-bd68-a65901c131c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r=z-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afbee862-c6a3-5534-8218-c408b82f6705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377669} :answer-field/id #uuid "b33bdfc5-dec0-53fc-8ace-b39805ac3670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f9c68a00-265c-5c7e-8372-6f380b398f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162332" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{p}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{q}{\\int }_{0}^{r}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(xy,z)}^{{u}_{4}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2},\\,\\,-2z\\le x\\le y+3}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/a4de9d9df51efcec67253a1c7118466f29459ffd5c9136c144d33500ec5557bc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/18bb3e55f076e022176bb60df27d5665ed2d91d75d618969c04ce192378db922.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=4-2z.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-2z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2z}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e61fa4c1-f85e-50ee-95a1-a3fd873a84b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt{y+1},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377675} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f88fab-2688-597a-8bbe-96692e1a56e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=-\\sqrt{2y+4},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377676} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ec9863-beb1-5e43-a02e-8cdccdc7680c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=-\\sqrt{2y+4},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377677} {:answer/id #uuid "9282627e-643f-5858-b3ec-29193e2885db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=-\\sqrt{2y}+1,\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{2y}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377678} {:answer/id #uuid "4c67a538-b238-5aa8-bcb5-b80bf0462251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt{y+1},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8ec9863-beb1-5e43-a02e-8cdccdc7680c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=-\\sqrt{2y+4},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377677} :answer-field/id #uuid "892c2155-f5fd-54fd-9250-1944f2eb22c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2a5a8c8-6950-5829-80bf-9e124f5f84d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162333" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{({x}^{2}-4)/2}^{(x+2)^{3}/4}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{p}^{0}{\\int }_{q}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{r}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,y)}^{{u}_{4}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-4)\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3},\\,\\,x\\le z\\le {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{({x}^{2}-4)/2}^{(x+2)^{3}/4}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/a38afc63496b487803590ccd57e2ab92ad4061524007e8bb4fb6277ff952dde2.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-4)\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/9bbaf6d9ce11ecbd988954365e9693f7f3f175c1dd0f944cc85b73773beca047.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=-\\sqrt{2y+4}\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 0,\\,-\\sqrt{2y+4}\\le x\\le 0}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2y+4}}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b6ff94a-8dbd-5742-8103-4b0009af2350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377682} {:answer/id #uuid "448c0181-b3a2-5bb6-ad92-331e6171afd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377683} {:answer/id #uuid "9081f8da-4c92-5709-842f-7383e6b987d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377684} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad62e73-0ac4-5622-8b1b-3341b990ad47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377685} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2b7763-cd08-5f28-99ec-eb653ec5d2b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448c0181-b3a2-5bb6-ad92-331e6171afd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377683} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9559dc4-5293-5631-a6a9-9caf40743ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96ed16b5-9286-5880-b845-c73c1331113c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162334" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{3}y}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/14c67512e143995cbfcfcf9e80c8d0975bbbc42368c97aa29956bd2d3f3454a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=1\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\,⟹\\,z={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le {e}^{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/d18470b45d21b4734187c1f639784a4e75924ed212e3fa4c7a214d19eaeb6022.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{3}y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\pi }{3}[z]_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\pi }{3}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}[{e}^{y}-y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(e-1-1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8168ba34-d7c2-549b-8c10-a1d3d2f5b62d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt[3]{x+2},\\,\\,\\,r=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377689} {:answer/id #uuid "8b95ba1a-490c-5097-affb-874efe154a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=(x+2)^{3},\\,\\,\\,r={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377690} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a5f30b-9aba-50cd-a015-55f1c1c1548d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt[3]{x+2},\\,\\,\\,r=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377691} {:answer/id #uuid "53062854-819e-5797-a8ea-2a39303974a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=3(x+2),\\,\\,\\,r=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377692} {:answer/id #uuid "e56a5336-c4aa-5c21-a1d1-a08776f3d140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=(x+2)^{3},\\,\\,\\,r=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56a5336-c4aa-5c21-a1d1-a08776f3d140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=(x+2)^{3},\\,\\,\\,r=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377693} :answer-field/id #uuid "1661af9e-d602-5459-9e37-c8d1bdef06b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e16ceaa2-a2b6-5fca-b0ad-53c38f3a9bbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162335" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}-2}^{y}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-3}^{p}{\\int }_{-1}^{q}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{r}^{1}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,y)}^{{u}_{4}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}-2\\le x\\le y,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le z\\le x+4}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}-2}^{y}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(x)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c92ab09dc71043ac3f5265202f36e38110e5e8db9f2dd115ab006721ed66d4c0.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}-2\\le x\\le y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/8ac0e7631d0baa8ba3b4c37ebe2b2b1d8efa650495d197f425aba093f342a9d3.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=-1\\,$ and $\\,y=(x+2)^{3}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=x\\,$ and $\\,y=1.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le (x+2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{(x+2)^{3}}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e85a6647-6a17-5de0-a1b6-ab16b8282c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377696} {:answer/id #uuid "80d7d9a1-4e52-5904-8b11-18df21911037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377697} {:answer/id #uuid "3a02f5fa-98ce-51ad-a008-c114c99b3a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377698} {:answer/id #uuid "84644858-9bde-5cce-a255-b7ec42f8a63c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377699} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3f14b0-efdb-5c0c-8038-86cdff16ce14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a02f5fa-98ce-51ad-a008-c114c99b3a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377698} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea5a9e7d-d0f8-5005-b714-79691a97451c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b94d854-420e-5d57-988d-2367c6e7514b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162376" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c72489a2d33a181fe7bc07d5cc017b93daccac51889cbb61edd64d4ee34429df.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/98aa4c0a436e4649ccd3d48a53fbf94b0341bf30b95745c4d052381680d223cf.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/47380c108c5b271761de2a027399bbd5981f2a9bdff21e498d73d0b430d070c7.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cb2015a-3b46-5e6b-904c-1d38c1310142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377703} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdd0468-c58a-5b5e-b04a-97e7ba719976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377704} {:answer/id #uuid "2076f531-0026-5c23-9b9b-c9daa53f5341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377705} {:answer/id #uuid "1af92249-c4df-50ef-bdd6-973c66f689a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377706} {:answer/id #uuid "540c103a-e9e9-5635-a3bf-d504994454e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2076f531-0026-5c23-9b9b-c9daa53f5341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377705} :answer-field/id #uuid "6631bb4a-11be-5954-8d9b-b5ec3051ef6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1c4060e-84a8-5bdb-9ef4-cbca501a104c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162383" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/5907cf8a9103cc9a639fd8078da3646cc0395d28827f58c0f1beef0a1c06645b.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/a89646433c8851f66c94092b75fae7b46b01f31c69ca03d338464b87bd7f5df4.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is \n\n$$\nx=y\\,⟹\\,y=x\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/1604e44050208cfb3b4dd81d35bff9769c7498d8304583d5ba92ad4b2f62fd05.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7ae675e-4ed8-571c-91e3-6f972fd96c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4/(xy)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377710} {:answer/id #uuid "c73526c2-ee4f-58f7-b19f-608dc444f564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377711} {:answer/id #uuid "3beb4534-bb8c-50b1-b430-50c8aa3256ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{-4/y}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4/(xy)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377712} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3deb3a-5f4d-5244-99b0-b13492ad6381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377713} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9518d5-11c7-5169-a67e-66e80075bd40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{-4/y}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73526c2-ee4f-58f7-b19f-608dc444f564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377711} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1043adb-69e6-5331-aeab-f995c028b8b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7fcce9c-e434-56cb-96bb-7fe09af48258" :question/math-academy-id "q-162406" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/fcc776a63cd1ac9fc5ec91157652ce9e75c6ee515eca8c6223309521cc300b61.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,-\\frac{4}{x}\\le y\\le -1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+\\frac{xy}{2}}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/1f30c28c7c048faae864da08a5a6d67d0c6346b27c00a9e7726234531f2f0783.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,-\\frac{4}{x}\\le y\\le -1}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{x}\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nand the left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le y\\le -1,\\,\\,1\\le x\\le -\\frac{4}{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/acc210f36bccb873526172149b85941bd42bc1648a464862562723fedca27bcb.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eccb936c-57e1-5cf6-9121-e886912a06ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377716} {:answer/id #uuid "5f352591-0adb-5f26-bbea-08288ae7d203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{1+y}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377717} {:answer/id #uuid "85f7a07c-1053-5951-9cd7-3865bb698565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{1+y-z}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377718} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddab9ae-39e2-5293-a9f3-bb7f2fe8e998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377719} {:answer/id #uuid "08b3c58a-fb85-5876-a670-a4ccb2e215d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{1+y-z}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eccb936c-57e1-5cf6-9121-e886912a06ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377716} :answer-field/id #uuid "acb02014-a370-53c0-8a33-e4ac60f0241c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b08713a3-4d0e-55df-a71c-6cc156f3ac58" :question/math-academy-id "q-162527" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/024df0fe0a965feba2177adfac8bbd658fc90f4775a4707f13eba1ef2cb94702.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z},\\,\\,1+y-z\\le x\\le 2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/8fff232443e6da697862c2e85a9d3abfe0554b80c2fcbfa8c93de42b94a45691.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{z}\\,⟹\\,z={y}^{3}\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,{y}^{3}\\le z\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/726ff420cae25c40f212714141a134fab84633cca7f49bdd268f0668d5248766.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}]}
